  if not exists (select 1 from reading_passages where title = $q$Coral Reefs and the Problem of Bleaching$q$) then
    insert into reading_passages(title, body, topic, difficulty, word_count, is_published, generation_batch)
      values ($q$Coral Reefs and the Problem of Bleaching$q$, $q$Coral reefs are often described as the rainforests of the sea because of their extraordinary richness. Although they cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, they support an enormous variety of marine life. A reef is built by tiny animals called coral polyps, which secrete hard skeletons of calcium carbonate over many generations. The bright colors of a healthy reef, however, do not come from the corals themselves. They come from microscopic algae that live inside the coral's tissues and supply it with most of its food through photosynthesis.

This partnership is delicate, and when the surrounding water becomes too warm, the coral expels its algae, and its white skeleton shows through the transparent tissue. The process is known as bleaching, and a bleached coral is not yet dead, but it has lost its main source of energy and becomes far more vulnerable to disease. If temperatures return to normal quickly, the algae may return and the coral can recover. If the heat persists for several weeks, the coral usually starves and eventually dies.

Bleaching events have become more frequent and more severe since the 1980s as ocean temperatures have risen. Australia's Great Barrier Reef, the largest reef system in the world, has suffered several mass bleaching events in recent years. Scientists are experimenting with ways to help reefs survive, such as breeding corals that tolerate heat and shading reefs during the hottest periods. Yet most researchers agree that such local measures can only buy a limited amount of time. The long-term future of coral reefs depends on limiting the warming of the oceans themselves.$q$, $q$environment$q$, 4, 267, true, $q$reading_shabbat_2026-09-05_A$q$)
      returning id into v_pid;
