  if not exists (select 1 from reading_passages where title = $q$The Human Microbiome$q$) then
    insert into reading_passages(title, body, topic, difficulty, word_count, is_published, generation_batch)
      values ($q$The Human Microbiome$q$, $q$The human body is home to trillions of microorganisms, including bacteria, fungi, and viruses, which together are known as the microbiome. The largest and most diverse community lives in the gut, but microbes also inhabit the skin, the mouth, and nearly every other surface of the body. For most of medical history, bacteria were viewed primarily as agents of disease. Only in recent decades have scientists come to appreciate how much the body depends on its microbial residents.

Gut microbes perform a range of tasks that human cells cannot manage on their own. They break down fibers that would otherwise pass through the digestive system undigested, releasing compounds that nourish the cells lining the intestine. They produce certain vitamins and help train the immune system to distinguish harmful invaders from harmless substances. A well-established community also occupies space and resources, making it harder for dangerous bacteria to gain a foothold.

The composition of the microbiome is shaped by diet, environment, and medical treatment, and it varies considerably from one person to another. Antibiotics, while essential for fighting infections, can dramatically reduce microbial diversity, sometimes for months. Researchers have linked disturbed microbial communities to a range of conditions, from digestive disorders to obesity and even mood. Establishing whether these associations reflect cause or consequence, however, has proved difficult.

The field is young, and enthusiasm sometimes runs ahead of the evidence: many products now claim to improve health by restoring beneficial bacteria, yet few have been tested rigorously. What is beyond doubt is that the microbiome is not a passenger but a participant in human biology. Understanding this hidden community may reshape how doctors think about nutrition, immunity, and disease.$q$, $q$science / biology$q$, 6, 278, true, $q$reading_shabbat_2026-09-05_A$q$)
      returning id into v_pid;
