  if not exists (select 1 from reading_passages where title = $q$Experiments with the Four-Day Week$q$) then
    insert into reading_passages(title, body, topic, difficulty, word_count, is_published, generation_batch)
      values ($q$Experiments with the Four-Day Week$q$, $q$The five-day work week is often treated as a natural law, yet it is less than a century old. In the 1920s, Henry Ford reduced the working week in his factories from six days to five without cutting pay, and other employers gradually followed. Since then, working hours in wealthy countries have fallen only slowly, even as productivity has multiplied. In recent years, a growing number of companies have asked whether the next step should be a four-day week.

One of the best-known experiments took place in 2018 at Perpetual Guardian, a New Zealand firm that manages trusts and estates. Its employees worked four days while receiving their full salary, and researchers monitored the results. Staff reported lower stress and better work-life balance, and the company found that productivity did not decline. In Iceland, large trials in the public sector between 2015 and 2019 reduced hours for thousands of workers with similar results. The largest pilot so far ran in Britain in 2022, when dozens of organizations tried the shorter week for six months. Most of them chose to continue afterward.

However, the evidence has clear limits. Participating firms volunteer for the trials, so they are probably more suited to the change than the average employer. The model is easier for office workers than for hospitals, factories, or shops that must stay open every day. Some employees in the trials also reported that their days became more intense as the same work was squeezed into less time. Whether the four-day week spreads widely or remains a benefit for a fortunate few is a question that only larger experiments can answer.$q$, $q$economics / society$q$, 4, 266, true, $q$reading_shabbat_2026-09-05_F$q$)
      returning id into v_pid;
