  if not exists (select 1 from reading_passages where title = $q$The Great Exhibition of 1851 and the Crystal Palace$q$) then
    insert into reading_passages(title, body, topic, difficulty, word_count, is_published, generation_batch)
      values ($q$The Great Exhibition of 1851 and the Crystal Palace$q$, $q$In 1851, London hosted the first international exhibition of manufactured goods, officially called the Great Exhibition of the Works of Industry of All Nations. The idea was promoted by Prince Albert, the husband of Queen Victoria, and by Henry Cole, a civil servant with a passion for design. Their aim was to display the products of modern industry from every part of the world in a single building. Britain, then the leading industrial power, expected to demonstrate its superiority, yet exhibits from dozens of other countries were also invited.

The building that housed the exhibition became as famous as the objects inside it. The organizers had rejected hundreds of conventional designs before Joseph Paxton, a gardener who had built large greenhouses, proposed a structure of iron and glass. Because its parts were manufactured in standard sizes and assembled on site, the enormous hall rose in Hyde Park in a matter of months. Visitors were astonished by the light-filled interior, which even enclosed several mature trees. A magazine soon nicknamed it the Crystal Palace, and the name stuck.

The exhibition attracted about six million visitors between May and October, an extraordinary number for the period. Prices were lowered on certain days so that ordinary workers could afford to attend, and railway companies organized cheap excursion trains from distant towns. Among the roughly one hundred thousand exhibits were steam engines, printing machines, textiles, and the famous Koh-i-Noor diamond from India.

The event's influence outlasted the exhibition itself. The substantial profits were used to purchase land in South Kensington, where museums devoted to science, natural history and the decorative arts were later built. The Crystal Palace was dismantled and rebuilt in south London, where it stood as a popular attraction until a fire destroyed it in 1936.$q$, $q$history$q$, 5, 292, true, $q$reading_shabbat_2026-09-05_G$q$)
      returning id into v_pid;
