  if not exists (select 1 from reading_passages where title = $q$Halley's Comet$q$) then
    insert into reading_passages(title, body, topic, difficulty, word_count, is_published, generation_batch)
      values ($q$Halley's Comet$q$, $q$Comets have fascinated and frightened people for thousands of years. Ancient observers often saw them as warnings of disaster, since they appeared suddenly and vanished without any obvious pattern. In the late seventeenth century, the English astronomer Edmond Halley set out to test this view. Using Newton's newly published theory of gravity, he calculated the orbits of two dozen comets that had been recorded in earlier centuries.

Halley noticed that the comets of 1531, 1607, and 1682 had followed strikingly similar paths through the sky. He argued that these were not three separate objects but a single comet returning roughly every seventy-six years. In 1705, he published a bold prediction that the same comet would appear again in 1758. Halley did not live to see the result, yet the comet returned on schedule and was named in his honor.

The success of the prediction transformed comets from omens into ordinary members of the solar system. Halley's Comet follows a long, stretched orbit that carries it far beyond Neptune before it swings back toward the sun. When it approaches, heat from the sun releases gas and dust from its icy nucleus, producing the glowing tail that observers see from Earth. Records of its appearances stretch back more than two thousand years, including a famous image woven into the Bayeux Tapestry after its visit in 1066.

The comet's most recent appearance came in 1986, when a fleet of spacecraft flew past it and photographed its nucleus for the first time. The images revealed a dark, irregular body only about fifteen kilometers long. Its next return is expected in 2061, and many people alive today will have the chance to see it. Few objects in the sky connect the science of the present so directly to the wonder of the past.$q$, $q$science / astronomy$q$, 4, 290, true, $q$reading_shabbat_2026-09-05_I$q$)
      returning id into v_pid;
    insert into reading_questions(passage_id, question_text, options, correct_option_index, explanations_he, question_type, window_size, highlight_spans, qa_note, display_order, is_published)
      values (v_pid, $q$An appropriate title for this text would be —$q$, $q$["The Spacecraft That Photographed a Comet in 1986", "Why Ancient Peoples Feared the Appearance of Comets", "How One Comet Changed from an Omen into a Predictable Visitor", "The Bayeux Tapestry and the Comet of 1066"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$["לא נכון. תצפית 1986 היא פרט אחד מהפסקה האחרונה בלבד.", "לא נכון. הפחד הקדום מוזכר רק בפתיחה, והקטע עוסק בעיקר בהפיכתו של השביט לגוף צפוי.", "נכון. הקטע עוקב אחרי השביט מאות מבשרות רעה ועד לגוף שמסלולו חושב ושובו נחזה, וזה הרעיון המרכזי.", "לא נכון. שטיח באיה מוזכר בפרט אחד בפסקה השלישית בלבד."]$q$::jsonb, $q$main_idea$q$, $q$text$q$, null, $q$shabbat batch I, passage=halley, q1$q$, 1, true);
    insert into reading_questions(passage_id, question_text, options, correct_option_index, explanations_he, question_type, window_size, highlight_spans, qa_note, display_order, is_published)
      values (v_pid, $q$According to the second paragraph, all of the following are true EXCEPT —$q$, $q$["Halley believed that three recorded comets were actually one object.", "Halley estimated that the comet returned about every seventy-six years.", "Halley's prediction was published more than fifty years before the expected return.", "Halley personally witnessed the comet's return in 1758 and was honored for it."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$["לא נכון. זו קביעה נכונה על פי הפסקה השנייה, ולכן אינה החריג המבוקש — האלי טען ששלושת השביטים הם גוף אחד.", "לא נכון. זו קביעה נכונה על פי הפסקה השנייה, ולכן אינה החריג המבוקש — הוא העריך שהשביט חוזר בערך כל שבעים ושש שנים.", "לא נכון. זו קביעה נכונה על פי הפסקה השנייה, ולכן אינה החריג המבוקש — התחזית פורסמה ב-1705 לקראת שובו ב-1758.", "נכון. זו הקביעה השגויה — הפסקה כותבת במפורש שהאלי לא זכה לראות את התוצאה, והשביט נקרא על שמו לאחר מותו."]$q$::jsonb, $q$explicit_detail$q$, $q$paragraph$q$, $q$["Halley did not live to see the result"]$q$::jsonb, $q$shabbat batch I, passage=halley, q2$q$, 2, true);
    insert into reading_questions(passage_id, question_text, options, correct_option_index, explanations_he, question_type, window_size, highlight_spans, qa_note, display_order, is_published)
      values (v_pid, $q$The main purpose of the third paragraph is to —$q$, $q$["describe the comet's orbit, its physical behavior, and its long history of observation.", "explain how Halley used Newton's theory to calculate the orbits of comets.", "report what spacecraft discovered about the comet's nucleus in 1986.", "argue that ancient fears of comets were not entirely unreasonable."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$["נכון. הפסקה מתארת את המסלול המוארך, את היווצרות הזנב ואת תיעוד ההופעות לאורך אלפיים שנה.", "לא נכון. זהו תוכן הפסקה הראשונה — חישובי האלי — ולא הפסקה השלישית.", "לא נכון. זהו תוכן הפסקה הרביעית — הצילומים מ-1986 — ולא הפסקה השלישית.", "לא נכון. הפסקה דווקא מדגישה שהשביטים הפכו מאותות לגופים רגילים במערכת השמש."]$q$::jsonb, $q$paragraph_purpose$q$, $q$paragraph$q$, null, $q$shabbat batch I, passage=halley, q3$q$, 3, true);
    insert into reading_questions(passage_id, question_text, options, correct_option_index, explanations_he, question_type, window_size, highlight_spans, qa_note, display_order, is_published)
      values (v_pid, $q$In the third paragraph, the word "omens" most nearly means —$q$, $q$["distant stars that shine with a steady light", "signs believed to foretell future events", "scientific instruments used for careful observation of the sky", "objects that travel around the sun in a fixed orbit"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$["לא נכון. המילה אינה מתארת כוכבים.", "נכון. 'omens' פירושו אותות שמאמינים שהם מבשרים אירועים עתידיים — בהתאם לפסקה הראשונה, שבה שביטים נתפסו כאזהרות מפני אסון.", "לא נכון. המילה אינה עוסקת במכשירים.", "לא נכון. זהו ההפך — הפסקה מנגידה בין אותות לבין גופים רגילים במסלול קבוע."]$q$::jsonb, $q$vocab_in_context$q$, $q$anchor$q$, $q$["transformed comets from omens into ordinary members of the solar system"]$q$::jsonb, $q$shabbat batch I, passage=halley, q4$q$, 4, true);
    insert into reading_questions(passage_id, question_text, options, correct_option_index, explanations_he, question_type, window_size, highlight_spans, qa_note, display_order, is_published)
      values (v_pid, $q$It can be inferred from the fourth paragraph that —$q$, $q$["the comet's nucleus is much larger than astronomers had previously believed.", "the comet will not be visible from Earth again for several centuries.", "before 1986, no one had obtained a direct picture of the comet's nucleus.", "the spacecraft that visited the comet in 1986 landed on its surface."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$["לא נכון. הפסקה מתארת גוף קטן יחסית, כחמישה עשר קילומטרים בלבד, ואינה משווה לציפיות קודמות.", "לא נכון. הפסקה כותבת שהשיבה הבאה צפויה ב-2061, בתוך חיי רבים מהחיים כיום.", "נכון. הפסקה כותבת שהחלליות צילמו את גרעין השביט 'בפעם הראשונה' ב-1986, ומכאן שלפני כן לא היה צילום ישיר שלו.", "לא נכון. הפסקה כותבת שהחלליות חלפו ליד השביט וצילמו אותו, לא שנחתו עליו."]$q$::jsonb, $q$inference$q$, $q$paragraph$q$, null, $q$shabbat batch I, passage=halley, q5$q$, 5, true);
  end if;

  if not exists (select 1 from reading_passages where title = $q$The Hubble Space Telescope's Flawed Mirror$q$) then
    insert into reading_passages(title, body, topic, difficulty, word_count, is_published, generation_batch)
      values ($q$The Hubble Space Telescope's Flawed Mirror$q$, $q$The Hubble Space Telescope was launched in April 1990 after decades of planning and a cost of well over a billion dollars. Placed above the Earth's atmosphere, it was expected to deliver images far sharper than any telescope on the ground could produce. Within weeks, however, the first pictures revealed a serious problem. Stars appeared as fuzzy halos rather than sharp points, and engineers soon traced the fault to the telescope's main mirror. Its surface had been ground to the wrong shape by a tiny amount, roughly one fiftieth of the width of a human hair.

The error was small in physical terms but devastating for the telescope's performance. A mirror gathers light and focuses it to a single point, and even a minute deviation spreads that light over a wider area. Investigators later found that a measuring instrument used during manufacturing had been assembled incorrectly. The mirror had been polished with great precision, but it had been polished precisely to the wrong specification. Because the flaw was uniform across the entire surface, it could, in principle, be corrected.

Replacing the mirror in orbit was impossible, so engineers designed a set of small corrective mirrors shaped to cancel the error exactly. In December 1993, astronauts on the space shuttle Endeavour installed the new optics during a series of demanding spacewalks. The repair succeeded completely, and Hubble went on to become one of the most productive scientific instruments ever built. Its images of distant galaxies changed estimates of the age of the universe and revealed the effects of dark energy. The telescope that began as an embarrassment ended as a symbol of what careful engineering can achieve.$q$, $q$technology / space$q$, 5, 274, true, $q$reading_shabbat_2026-09-05_I$q$)
      returning id into v_pid;
